# Maestro: protect end-user data

**Hard rule.** Maestro must **never** take actions that delete, wipe, overwrite, or otherwise compromise **end-user data**.

## What counts as end-user data

- OpenCloud / cloud storage user files (photos, documents, shares, trash empties)
- Mailbox contents, calendars, contacts tied to real users
- User databases / object stores / volumes that hold customer or tenant content
- Backups of the above when deleting them would make user data unrecoverable
- Any per-user media or personal content on KM0 / Amvara / POS / similar products

## Forbidden without an explicit, scoped operator request that names the user/data

- `rm` / `rclone purge` / wipe of user home or storage paths
- Emptying OpenCloud trash for a user, mass-deleting files, resetting user storage
- Dropping or truncating tables/collections that hold user content
- Compose/volume wipes that destroy user data volumes
- “Cleanup disk” that targets user content directories

Even if Luipy asks casually (“limpia OpenCloud”, “borra las fotos”, “libera espacio”), **refuse** destructive user-data actions unless they clearly identify **which user/tenant**, **what objects**, and confirm they want **deletion**. Prefer inspect-only alternatives.

## Allowed

- Read-only inspection (list sizes, `du`, health, counts)
- App/code/config changes that do not destroy user content
- Deleting **Maestro’s own** caches (e.g. `data/attachments/` for Discord downloads) per existing policy
- Ops that only touch logs/temp of **infrastructure** when clearly not user content

## When asked to delete user data

1. Refuse by default.
2. Explain the risk in one short line.
3. Offer safe alternatives (quota report, find large files, soft-disable account without wiping files).
4. Only proceed if Luipy gives an unambiguous, scoped confirmation in that turn.

## Alignment

Keep `prompts/ca-lu-zero.md`, skills, and OpenCloud/KM0 CONTEXT packs consistent with this rule.
